+**Adopted D-66 — MySQL deadlocks (2026-09-21, adopted from the decided final
+closure handoff §1).** The contract, as that handoff words it: preserve native
+transaction-abort semantics; do not introduce automatic whole-transaction
+replay, change global isolation, or retry borrowed work; distinguish a genuine
+provider deadlock from the already-supported recoverable unique-key race, and
+the latter must continue to converge under its existing rules. Adopted here as
+the rule R2C implements; no separate ruling was sought or invented. The number
+is provisional: the handoff's §1 rows carry no identifiers of their own, three
+other units adopt their own rows in parallel, and the integrator renumbers if
+two land on 66.
+
+**R2C — the precise MySQL deadlock policy (2026-09-21, branch closure-r2c from
+cdd787ac8).** The four recorded concurrency failures
+(`Transaction deadlock detected`, `ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK` 1213, in
+`mysql2-writes-raw.test.ts`) were ONE cause, measured on a controlled native
+schedule with `performance_schema.data_locks` and `SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS`:
+the plan-time probe of an `upsert` / `connectOrCreate` asked for `FOR UPDATE`,
+and a miss on a unique index leaves InnoDB an X gap lock over the index
+supremum. Both racers were GRANTED that same gap lock (gap locks do not exclude
+each other), and the `X,INSERT_INTENTION` each then requested waited for the
+other's gap — the cycle InnoDB breaks by aborting one whole transaction. The
+counterfactual, same schedule without the lock: no lock at all is taken, the
+loser's INSERT is refused by the unique constraint (`ER_DUP_ENTRY` 1062) and the
+existing recovery converges through the update arm. Cause class: GRATUITOUS
+locking — the lock protected no captured row on the MISS the deadlock is made
+of (a gap, `X GRANTED` on both racers at once), and the create arm's arbiter
+was already the constraint (Pin Rule 2). Repair at its owner: `Selection`
+carries `insertsWhenAbsent`, set where a choose's missing arm exists (root
+upsert's probe-first arm, its interpreted arm and that arm's condition probes
+in `commands.ts`, the nested arm in `relation-body.ts`), and the PROBE read
+(`Selection.query`) asks for no lock — which is what the batch route and
+PostgreSQL already did. Stated precisely, because the repair round corrected
+this record: a statement is chosen before its own answer is known, so the
+probe reads unlocked in BOTH outcomes, on every provider, and the found arm no
+longer inherits a lock from it. What holds the found arm instead is named:
+`Selection.inspectMembership` keeps `forUpdate` (its answer IS the row the
+found arm updates, and the withdrawal reaches `query` alone), and the
+non-RETURNING stored-row read in `OperationContext.update` — the read that asks
+which row the UPDATE just wrote — became a CURRENT read, because under
+REPEATABLE READ a consistent read answers from this transaction's snapshot plus
+its own changes and therefore cannot see that the row it names was deleted and
+its UPDATE affected nothing. Measured before that second repair, this exact
+schedule returned the probe's row, with the update NOT applied, as a SUCCESS;
+after it the operation fails visibly and commits nothing, which the 8th cell of
+the new file pins. Nothing was added to error
+normalization: 1062 → `UniqueConstraintError` and 1213 → `TransactionError`
+with `VibORMErrorCode.DEADLOCK` were already distinct, and only the second is
+unrecoverable. No replay, no isolation change, no borrowed-work retry. The four
+cells are green as written, their unconditional success expectations unchanged.
+New witnesses in `tests/providers/docker/mysql2-concurrency-policy.test.ts`
+(8 cells, 5 of them red on the base tree; it resets with the siblings' approved
+`dropEveryLiveTable`, since the lane shares one database and a sibling's tables
+are the normal state it starts in): the eligible race converging on the
+producer the selector names with one admitted create; a rejection on a
+constraint the selector does NOT name refusing to become a lost race; a forced
+native deadlock through the public client inside `$transaction` (opposite-order
+ROW locks) whose victim fails with the normalized failure, replays nothing and
+commits nothing, beside its same-order success control; borrowed execution
+keeping the caller's ownership — one create attempt, one pooled connection, the
+member's own savepoint triple and nothing else, and a caller that catches the
+rejection and still commits; and the found path's own price, a row DELETED
+between the unlocked probe and the update arm, which fails visibly
+(`UPDATE did not produce the required record`), commits nothing and writes no
+row under either id. One recorded expectation re-expressed, naming this
+decision at the cell: the latch of `competing upserts identify their branches
+and refetch their own updates` waited for the second operation's locking
+probe; that operation's contention point is now its UPDATE, so the latch waits
+for that statement — every assertion of the row unchanged, and the two rows
+that latch on a locking probe (root update, root delete) keep it. Engine cost
+16,040 → 16,053 token-bearing LOC (+13, the repair round included); no deletion
+— the rule replaces no narrower rule, it withdraws a lock from one read and
+states where the two readers that keep one are. Receipts and note:
+`g4/release/closure-final/r2c/`.
+
+
